{"causes": ["The fragmentation of Okinawa Island into three competing kingdoms (Hokuzan, Chūzan, Nanzan) created an unstable political environment.", "The Ming Dynasty's tributary system incentivized Okinawan rulers to seek external legitimization and trade advantages.", "The strategic ambitions and military prowess of Shō Hashi, ruler of the Chūzan Kingdom, drove the unification efforts across Okinawa."], "effects": ["Han'anchi's defeat led to the collapse of the Sanhoku Kingdom, eliminating one of the three major polities on Okinawa Island.", "The fall of Sanhoku directly enabled Shō Hashi to consolidate power and unify Okinawa, ending the Sanzan period.", "His demise contributed to the eventual establishment of the unified Ryukyu Kingdom and the First Shō Dynasty in 1429."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "shō-hashi", "sourceName": "Shō Hashi", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "hananchi", "targetName": "Hananchi", "context": "Shō Hashi, as ruler of the Chūzan Kingdom, caused the downfall and defeat of Han'anchi's Sanhoku Kingdom around 1416."}, {"sourceSlug": "hananchi", "sourceName": "Hananchi", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "hokuzan-castle", "targetName": "Hokuzan Castle", "context": "Han'anchi's seat of power as King of Sanhoku was Hokuzan Castle (Nakijin Castle) in northern Okinawa."}, {"sourceSlug": "ming-dynasty", "sourceName": "Ming Dynasty", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "hananchi", "targetName": "Hananchi", "context": "The Ming Dynasty influenced Han'anchi through its tributary system, which he participated in from 1396 to 1415."}, {"sourceSlug": "hananchi", "sourceName": "Hananchi", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "sanzan-period", "targetName": "Sanzan Period", "context": "Han'anchi participated in the Sanzan period as one of the three competing kings on Okinawa Island before its unification."}, {"sourceSlug": "hananchi", "sourceName": "Hananchi", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "min", "targetName": "Min", "context": "Han'anchi succeeded Min as the King of Sanhoku, though their exact blood relationship remains unknown to historians."}], "places": [{"name": "Hokuzan Castle, Okinawa, Japan", "role": "Seat of power"}, {"name": "Okinawa Island, Japan", "role": "Primary domain"}, {"name": "Naha, Okinawa, Japan", "role": "Rival power center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Hananchi", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "hananchi"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q888537", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Han%27anchi"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Han'anchi's defeat as the last King of Sanhoku directly paved the way for the unification of Okinawa under Shō Hashi, ending the Sanzan period and establishing the unified Ryukyu Kingdom that endured for over four centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-28T09:12:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46129142141", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Han'anchi (攀安知) was a local ruler of Okinawa Island, who was given the title of King of Sanhoku. Contemporary sources on Han'anchi are very scarce. He first appeared in Chinese diplomatic records in 1396 and his last contact was of 1415. His blood relationship with Min, the preceding King of Sanhoku, is unknown. After 1415, the King of Sanhoku did not contact China again. The Chinese records suggest that the Chinese had no information on when and how the king disappeared. Because the King of Chūzan continued tributary missions, the Chinese later speculated that the Kings of Sannan and Sanhoku had been removed by the King of Chūzan.", "newValue": "Han'anchi (攀安知) reigned as the final monarch of the Sanhoku (Hokuzan) Kingdom on Okinawa Island, a pivotal figure whose defeat in 1416 marked the beginning of Okinawa's unification under the Chūzan Kingdom. His rule, shrouded in historical ambiguity due to scarce contemporary records, represents the twilight of the Sanzan period, an era of three competing polities—Hokuzan, Chūzan, and Nanzan—vying for control of the Ryukyu archipelago. Appearing in Chinese diplomatic records from 1396 to 1415, Han'anchi's interactions with the Ming Dynasty underscore the importance of the tributary system in legitimizing Okinawan rulers and facilitating regional trade, even as internal strife intensified.

Han'anchi's reign was characterized by a precarious balance of power, with his kingdom centered at Nakijin Castle (also known as Hokuzan Castle) in the northern part of Okinawa Island. Chinese accounts confirm his status as King of Sanhoku, though they offer no details on his lineage or succession from the preceding King Min. His last recorded contact with the Ming court in 1415, followed by the complete cessation of Sanhoku's diplomatic missions, strongly suggests his downfall occurred around 1416. This period witnessed escalating military pressure from the southern Chūzan Kingdom, led by the ambitious Shō Hashi, who systematically dismantled his rivals to consolidate power.

The fall of Han'anchi and the Sanhoku Kingdom was a definitive turning point, directly paving the way for the unification of Okinawa under Shō Hashi. This conquest eliminated the last major obstacle to Chūzan's dominance, effectively ending the Sanzan period and laying the groundwork for the establishment of the unified Ryukyu Kingdom in 1429. Han'anchi's defeat thus represents not merely the end of a single reign, but the collapse of a centuries-old political fragmentation, ushering in an era of centralized rule that would define Okinawan history for over four centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-28T09:12:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46129142141", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1400"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1400"}], "newValue": ["The fragmentation of Okinawa Island into three competing kingdoms (Hokuzan, Chūzan, Nanzan) created an unstable political environment.", "The Ming Dynasty's tributary system incentivized Okinawan rulers to seek external legitimization and trade advantages.", "The strategic ambitions and military prowess of Shō Hashi, ruler of the Chūzan Kingdom, drove the unification efforts across Okinawa."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-28T09:12:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46129142141", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1416"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1416"}], "newValue": ["Han'anchi's defeat led to the collapse of the Sanhoku Kingdom, eliminating one of the three major polities on Okinawa Island.", "The fall of Sanhoku directly enabled Shō Hashi to consolidate power and unify Okinawa, ending the Sanzan period.", "His demise contributed to the eventual establishment of the unified Ryukyu Kingdom and the First Shō Dynasty in 1429."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-28T09:12:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46129142141", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "hananchi", "sourceName": "Hananchi", "verb": "RULES", "targetSlug": "field-221", "targetName": "Monarchs & Rulers", "context": "Hananchi: Sovereign authority in monarchs & rulers"}, {"sourceSlug": "hananchi", "sourceName": "Hananchi", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "medieval", "targetName": "Medieval Era", "context": "Hananchi was active during the Medieval era"}], "newValue": [{"sourceSlug": "shō-hashi", "sourceName": "Shō Hashi", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "hananchi", "targetName": "Hananchi", "context": "Shō Hashi, as ruler of the Chūzan Kingdom, caused the downfall and defeat of Han'anchi's Sanhoku Kingdom around 1416."}, {"sourceSlug": "hananchi", "sourceName": "Hananchi", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "hokuzan-castle", "targetName": "Hokuzan Castle", "context": "Han'anchi's seat of power as King of Sanhoku was Hokuzan Castle (Nakijin Castle) in northern Okinawa."}, {"sourceSlug": "ming-dynasty", "sourceName": "Ming Dynasty", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "hananchi", "targetName": "Hananchi", "context": "The Ming Dynasty influenced Han'anchi through its tributary system, which he participated in from 1396 to 1415."}, {"sourceSlug": "hananchi", "sourceName": "Hananchi", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "sanzan-period", "targetName": "Sanzan Period", "context": "Han'anchi participated in the Sanzan period as one of the three competing kings on Okinawa Island before its unification."}, {"sourceSlug": "hananchi", "sourceName": "Hananchi", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "min", "targetName": "Min", "context": "Han'anchi succeeded Min as the King of Sanhoku, though their exact blood relationship remains unknown to historians."}]}, {"ti